  <h2>Our editorial purpose</h2>
  <p>SmartAbodeLab exists to publish useful, technically grounded information for people making decisions about connected homes, energy use, appliances, and urban living. We aim to explain trade-offs rather than optimize content for clicks alone.</p>

  <h2>Authorship: who created the article</h2>
  <p>Articles should carry an accurate byline. Author pages describe the topics an author covers and, where applicable, their relevant experience, credentials, or external profiles. We do not use fictional expert identities or attribute work to a person who did not meaningfully contribute to it.</p>

  <h2>Research and sourcing</h2>
  <p>Writers should prefer primary and authoritative sources when they are available. Depending on the topic, this may include government agencies, standards organizations, academic research, technical documentation, manufacturers, utilities, and first-hand measurements.</p>
  <p>Secondary sources can add context, but should not replace an accessible primary source for a factual claim when the primary source is available.</p>

  <h2>Editorial review and fact checking</h2>
  <p>Not every article requires a separate reviewer. When a page says it was editorially reviewed or fact checked, that label means another identified person reviewed the published material for the stated purpose. The article can show the reviewer and review date.</p>

  <h2>How content was created</h2>
  <p>Where useful to the reader, an article can identify its creation method: editorial research, expert analysis, first-hand experience, or hands-on testing. A methodology note should explain important limitations, sample sizes, tools, test conditions, or other details that could affect interpretation.</p>

  <h2>AI and automation</h2>
  <p>AI tools may assist research organization, transcription, outlining, editing, coding, or other production tasks. AI assistance does not remove human editorial responsibility. When automation materially contributes to content in a way readers would reasonably want to know, the article should disclose that use.</p>
  <p>AI systems should not be presented as the author of an article. A human editor remains accountable for the accuracy, framing, sourcing, and final publication decision.</p>

  <h2>Editorial independence</h2>
  <p>Editorial conclusions should not be changed to satisfy an advertiser, affiliate partner, manufacturer, or other commercial party. Products do not receive favorable coverage merely because a commercial relationship exists.</p>

  <h2>Updates and corrections</h2>
  <p>Articles may be updated as standards, products, software, prices, regulations, or evidence change. Minor copy edits do not require a correction notice. Material factual corrections should be disclosed on the article with the date and a concise explanation of what changed.</p>

  <h2>Conflicts and disclosure</h2>
  <p>Authors and editors should disclose relationships that could reasonably affect a reader's assessment of their independence. Affiliate links and commercial relationships are handled under our <a href="/affiliate-disclosure">Affiliate Disclosure</a>.</p>
</EditorialPageLayout>
